/**
 * Launch a vessel.
 * @param connection A connection object.
 * @param craftDirectory Name of the directory in the current saves
 * "Ships" directory, that contains the craft file.
 * For example `"VAB"` or `"SPH"`.
 * @param name Name of the vessel to launch. This is the name of the ".craft" file
 * in the save directory, without the ".craft" file extension.
 * @param launchSite Name of the launch site. For example `"LaunchPad"` or
 * `"Runway"`.
 * @param crew The Kerbals to place in the craft, by name. Controls how the craft is
 * crewed, as described in the remarks.
 * @param recover If true and there is a vessel on the launch site,
 * recover it before launching.
 * @param flagUrl If not `null`, the asset URL of the mission flag to use for the launch.
 *
 * The crew parameter controls how the craft is crewed:
 *
 * - If `null`, the craft's default crew assignments are used.
 * - If an empty list, the craft is launched with no crew.
 * - Otherwise, the named Kerbals are placed in the craft in the order
 *   given: the first name takes the first seat, the second name the next seat, and so on,
 *   scanning the craft's parts in order. No other Kerbals are hired to fill remaining seats.
 *   Throws an exception, without launching, if a name does not match an available Kerbal
 *   or the craft has too few seats for the requested crew.
 *
 * Throws an exception if any of the games pre-flight checks fail.
 */
static inline krpc_error_t krpc_SpaceCenter_LaunchVessel(krpc_connection_t connection, const char * craftDirectory, const char * name, const char * launchSite, const krpc_list_string_t * crew, bool recover, const char * flagUrl);

